00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.640
is being collected
on the back end.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.360
And API management
platforms like Apigee

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.850
have a great
opportunity to leverage

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:31.470
this data to actually
help customers, one,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.860
improve the availability
and performance of the APIs,

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.460
and number two, to also ensure
that the APIs are secure

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.720
and meet the compliance
requirements.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.810
So across the
entire value chain,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.470
all the way from
your target back

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.240
end to front end
consumers, there

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.640
is this large amount of
metadata that Apigee is also

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.750
collecting at the back end.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:56.430
So if you look at the Apigee
API Management Platform today,

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.630
it is Google Cloud comprehensive
full lifecycle management

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:05.460
platform, which includes a
runtime layer, a mediation

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:11.250
engine, API analytics, and also
provides you a developer portal

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.030
and monetization capability.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.840
But the way we are
leveraging the API data today

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.657
is by providing three
different kind of metrics

00:05:19.657 --> 00:05:20.865
for three different personas.

00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:23.880
So for somebody like
the product manager,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:27.270
we provide developer
engagement metrics,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.730
and for a business
persona, we are

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.280
looking at business
metrics such as revenue

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.930
generation, et cetera.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.750
And for an operations
persona, today, we

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:41.170
have deeply integrated
monitoring capabilities.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.600
What we are introducing today
is API Security Reporting,

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.050
which is a new added
functionality which is also

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.600
leveraging the same API data.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.150
What we have seen in
most of our customers

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.300
is that, depending on the size
of the customer-- depending

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.980
on the nature of
their business--

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.820
they have either an ops person
looking into the security

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.660
attributes, or there is a SecOps
person dedicatedly looking

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.680
at the security attributes.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.850
So this feature
essentially targets both

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:16.470
the current IT admin or
office persona and also

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.110
the SecOps persona.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.550
So let's learn a little
bit about this new feature

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.660
that we just added.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.820
What is API Security Reporting?

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.090
It provides deep insights
for operations teams

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.250
to basically do three
very important things.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.750
One is to ensure
that all of the APIs

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.180
that are being developed
by the development teams

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.510
are adhering to the security
and compliance requirements.

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.700
Number two is to ensure
that the sensitive data

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.180
within the organization
is not abused

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.730
by internal or external users.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.340
And number three is when
there is an incident,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.880
it provides you the
capabilities to quickly

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.400
identify and diagnose
the root cause

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.460
and quickly take remedial
actions to solve it.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:06.360
Let's get into each of these
functionalities in detail.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.730
Security compliance
is essentially

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.420
a functionality that
addresses the design time

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.740
governance of the product.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.160
It is about ensuring
that developers

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.220
who are the core power
users of our platform

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.100
are building APIs
that incorporate

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:27.050
all the policies required
by the compliance teams.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.140
In many cases, we
have seen customers

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:34.310
who think that the APIs
that are being built

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:38.030
are by default adhering
to all the policies.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:40.880
But only when something
breaks, they actually

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.510
realize that that's not really
happening on the field, right?

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.880
With this feature,
users can quickly

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:55.430
identify proxies that do not
adhere to traffic security

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.980
and extension
policy requirements,

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.580
and users will also be
able to very quickly review

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.710
what are the shared
flow configurations

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.500
and revisions for
each of those proxies.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:09.800
And you can also
analyze which hosts,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.140
which virtual
hosts and ports are

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.990
open to receiving
secure traffic,

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.900
as compared to unsecure traffic.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.320
As we discussed earlier,
several enterprises

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.340
are today covering business
critical applications

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:26.690
through APIs.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.300
So there is a lot
of sensitive data

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.030
that is actually
involved in these APIs.

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.390
For example, developer
information, trade sessions,

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.860
API keys--

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:41.480
all of these are
attributes that are

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.960
vulnerable to abuse from
internal and external users.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.910
So with this new feature,
users can very closely

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.670
monitor who within
your organization

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:56.300
is actually accessing this
sensitive information,

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.860
and if there are any anomalies
in that access [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:05.540
you can discover or detect
those suspicious behavior.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:09.980
Apigee by default also
provides ways for you

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:13.070
to extract information
outside of the platform

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.560
through management
APIs, and it becomes

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:19.610
extremely critical for your
operations teams to make sure--

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.360
to monitor those users who
are actually extracting

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:26.040
this data out of the platform.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.390
So on top of all the
logging capabilities

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.630
that Apigee offers
today, data protection

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:35.720
is one additional
feature that helps

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.750
you to look at all
the user activity that

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:41.350
is happening inside your ops.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:43.855
And the third capability
that we've got

00:09:43.855 --> 00:09:46.420
is precision diagnosis.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.350
Precision diagnosis essentially
address the runtime governance

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.330
with software security.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:57.190
It gives you the insights into
what percentage of traffic

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:02.230
is secure versus nonsecure,
and when there is a security

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.200
incident, you can
quickly analyze

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.130
which apps are causing
those security incidents

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.890
and which back end targets
are actually getting

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.560
affected because of that.

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.310
You can also identify
anomalies in traffic patterns,

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:21.250
identify the root causes
for those anomalies,

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:24.490
and basically take the most
appropriate remedial actions

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:25.540
to solve this.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.910
What is happening
with this capability

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.830
is that a lot of
operations teams

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:33.070
find it very useful
in terms of overall

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.390
reducing their overall mean time
to diagnose an issue, and also

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:40.330
the mean time to take
remedial actions.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.840
So with that, let's just take
a quick break from the slides

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.110
and actually go into the
product and take a quick demo.

