00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:02.050
see the sensitive
operations of the user.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:03.478
So what are
sensitive operations?

00:14:03.478 --> 00:14:05.395
The way they have defined
sensitive operations

00:14:05.395 --> 00:14:09.550
is when a user is
accessing information

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:13.570
that contains either
developer information, app

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:19.120
information, virtual hosts,
trace sessions, or custom

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:19.990
reports.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:24.220
And let's say if there is
one particular user who

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.920
has accessed a lot
of sensitive data,

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:29.590
and if you feel
that the user is not

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:31.510
supposed to be
accessing that, you

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:34.150
can very quickly
contact that user

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:38.730
and take the most
appropriate action.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.880
What we also have here
is the Compliance page.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.250
This is the design
time governance aspect

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.900
that I was talking
about earlier.

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.260
So here, the first
thing that you can do

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:55.433
is look at the
various environments

00:14:55.433 --> 00:14:57.850
and select the right environment
that you want to look in.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.770
And then you will see the
list of all the proxies,

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:10.990
as well as their relevant
provision number against that.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.390
And what you also see
here is the number

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.520
of policies that
have been configured

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:17.080
for each of these proxies.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:21.880
These policies include traffic
policy, security policy,

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.340
as well as the extension policy.

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.200
What you will also
see is the number

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:27.910
of shared flows
that are configured

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:30.430
for each of the proxies,
and you will also

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:34.210
be able to see whether this
particular proxy has been

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:37.300
receiving secure traffic
HTTPS or if there's

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:40.540
unsecure traffic on non-HTTPS.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:44.020
And let's click on
one of these and see

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:46.250
what additional information
you can see here.

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:48.650
So when you click on this,
on the right hand panel,

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:52.450
you will also see the
details of the policies that

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:55.360
have been configured
against these policies.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.330
On the Shared Flows,
you will basically

00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:00.850
see the list of all
the shared flows that

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:04.060
have been configured, and when
you select that shared flow,

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.400
you will also see
the policies that

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:10.950
are configured against this
particular shared flow.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.270
And what we also have
here is the Target view.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.840
And Target view
essentially will give you

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.926
information about that
particular [AUDIO OUT]

00:16:22.926 --> 00:16:27.070
has received secure traffic
or if it's nonsecure traffic.

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:29.530
In several cases,
we have also seen

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:33.670
that customers have realized
that some of the targets

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:38.050
have been configured to
receive non-HTTPS traffic,

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:40.650
and after using
this feature enough,

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.150
they kind of very quickly
were able to change

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.850
the configurations to
ensure that it receives

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:48.590
only the secure traffic.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:52.850
And the third component that I
want to talk about is runtime.

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:56.560
This is the runtime
governance aspect.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:01.930
And just like compliance, you
can also select the environment

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:03.500
that you want to look at.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.680
You can change
the timeline here.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:11.609
And you can also select regions,
if you have multi regions,

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:16.020
and look at the
traffic that is flowing

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.520
into each of these proxies.

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:21.720
So what you see here is the
traffic over the selected

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:24.569
period of time and
the percentage change,

00:17:24.569 --> 00:17:29.100
and what percentage is
HTTPS versus non-HTTPS.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:31.320
You will also be
able to quickly see

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.890
what other aspects are
accessing this proxy,

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:37.270
and if there is
any of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:39.210
So let's take a
situation and understand

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:40.650
this a little better.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:44.070
If there is a high
percentage change

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:47.280
in the traffic that is
coming to a particular proxy,

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.270
and [INAUDIBLE] I add an ops
person, I add a SecOps person.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.120
I understand that this is
something that is not supposed

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:58.350
to happen, and that that is
coming mostly from a nonsecure

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:00.960
forward, then I
will be very quickly

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.610
able to understand which
app is causing the traffic.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.070
And once I know that,
I'll be able to take

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.660
the most remedial actions.

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.780
And to mitigate the
damage that is happening,

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:17.430
I can also look at which target
is actually being affected

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.880
because of the [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:23.930
So this kind of significantly
reduces the overall time

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.060
to understand what
is going wrong

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:30.760
with that particular proxy, and
take the most remedial action.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:34.170
So that is all about
the new security

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.120
reporting feature
that we launched.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.960
If you have any
further questions,

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.910
please feel free
to reach out to us.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:42.750
And if you are not an
Apigee Edge customer,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:45.620
please do download the free
trial and start using this.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:48.600
Thank you so much for joining
us, and have a nice afternoon.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

